::GEO_U06_L03_GP01::
Question ID: GEO_U06_L03_GP01
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.CGM.10
Question:
A coordinate rectangle has length 8 units and width 5 units. What is its area?
{
=A. 40 square units#Area of a rectangle is length times width, so 8(5)\=40.
~B. 26 square units#That is the perimeter, not the area.
~C. 13 square units#This adds the dimensions instead of multiplying.
~D. 80 square units#This doubles the correct area without a reason.
}

::GEO_U06_L03_GP02::
Question ID: GEO_U06_L03_GP02
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.CGM.13
Question:
A triangular garden has base 12 ft and height 7 ft. What is its area?
{
~A. 84 ft?#This omits the one-half factor for triangle area.
=B. 42 ft?#Triangle area is one-half base times height, so 1/2(12)(7)\=42.
~C. 19 ft?#This adds base and height instead of finding area.
~D. 38 ft?#This does not match the triangle area formula.
}

::GEO_U06_L03_GP03::
Question ID: GEO_U06_L03_GP03
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.CGM.10
Question:
Which method can find the area of an irregular coordinate polygon?
{
~A. Use only the perimeter#Perimeter measures distance around the figure, not enclosed area.
~B. Count only the vertices#The number of vertices does not determine area.
=C. Decompose the polygon into rectangles and triangles#Breaking the figure into familiar shapes gives areas that can be added.
~D. Use the largest x-coordinate#One coordinate value alone does not determine area.
}

::GEO_U06_L03_GP04::
Question ID: GEO_U06_L03_GP04
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.CGM.13
Question:
Which unit is appropriate for the area of a painted wall?
{
~A. feet#Feet are linear units, used for length.
~B. cubic feet#Cubic feet measure volume.
~C. degrees#Degrees measure angles, not area.
=D. square feet#Area is measured in square units such as square feet.
}

::GEO_U06_L03_GP05::
Question ID: GEO_U06_L03_GP05
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.CGM.10
Question:
A coordinate square has side length 6 units. What is its perimeter?
{
=A. 24 units#Perimeter of a square is 4s, so 4(6)\=24.
~B. 36 square units#That is area, not perimeter.
~C. 12 units#This doubles one side instead of adding all four sides.
~D. 18 units#This does not include all four equal sides.
}

